{"created": "2026-08-03T12:05:09.560952+00:00", "modified": "2026-08-05T09:21:45.894804+00:00", "id": 21773363, "conceptrecid": "21713860", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21773363", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.21713860", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21773363", "metadata": {"title": "The Consequence Boundary: Aurora-Lens and the Architecture of Admissibility Before AI Action", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21773363", "publication_date": "2026-08-03", "description": "<p>This report presents Aurora-Lens as a provider-agnostic runtime governance architecture for the boundary at which candidate model output becomes consequential advice, a decision, a record entry, an instruction, or an action.</p>\n<p>Aurora-Lens evaluates candidate output against persistent committed state and governing conditions before release. It supports deterministic admissibility evaluation, explicit unresolved outcomes, authority freshness and revocation, governed non-commitment, terminal refusal, and contemporaneous evidence of what was admitted, revised, contained, or stopped.</p>\n<p>The report distinguishes runtime admissibility from model evaluation, policy management, retrospective observability, and record integrity. It also examines the architecture's relevance to ISO/IEC 42001, the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, regulated enterprise deployment, licensing, acquisition, and technical diligence.</p>\n<p>A related-work analysis situates Aurora-Lens alongside the independently developed work of Paul Knowles, Derk Kraai, Nj&aring;l Gaute Solland, Ishaan Ghosh, Shawn Wright, Greg Malpass, and DirectTrust. Their respective contributions address action-specific containment, authorization decay, execution control, write custody, compiled organisational authority, governed memory, and autonomous-systems governance.</p>\n<p>The report includes a dated public chronology, implementation evidence, explicit limitations, evaluation questions, and a glossary. It does not claim that runtime evidence establishes normative authority, legal compliance, safe deployment, or institutional conformity by itself.</p>\n<p>This corrected edition, dated 1 August 2026, consolidates the related-work analysis around evidence-bearing architectural comparisons, tightens capability and chronology language, and replaces and directly credits Figure 7.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Stokes, Margaret", "affiliation": null, "orcid": "0009-0004-6422-4174"}], "keywords": ["AI governance", "runtime governance", "Aurora-Lens", "consequence boundary", "admissibility", "commitment control", "governed non-commitment", "deterministic governance", "persistent state", "authority validation", "execution control", "runtime evidence", "agentic AI", "ISO/IEC 42001", "AI assurance"], "resource_type": {"title": "Report", "type": "publication", "subtype": "report"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 1, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "21713860"}}]}}, "title": "The Consequence Boundary: Aurora-Lens and the Architecture of Admissibility Before AI Action", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21773363", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21773363", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21773363?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21773363", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21773363", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.21773363", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21773363/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21713860", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21713860", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21713860", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.21713860", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:21773363/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:21773363/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21773363/files", "m